(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-venous-disorders-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Venous Disorders Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 59-year-old man is about to start hemodialysis through a newly created forearm arteriovenous fistula. Which self-check should he be taught to confirm that the fistula is working?","choices":["Confirm the skin over it is cool","Confirm no sound is heard over it","Feel for a vibrating thrill","Confirm it stays full when raised"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A healthy fistula has a diffuse thrill and a soft bruit, so he can feel the buzzing over the access. Loss of the thrill signals a problem and should be reported promptly. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman on hemodialysis with a large upper arm arteriovenous fistula has developed exertional dyspnea, orthopnea and bilateral leg edema over several weeks. The fistula has a strong thrill (a vibration felt over the access). Which complication of the fistula should be suspected?","choices":["Lymphedema of the arm","Heart failure","Superficial thrombophlebitis","Deep vein thrombosis of the leg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A large arteriovenous fistula shunts blood past the capillary bed, and the examination of these patients should watch for heart failure, along with infection, chronic venous insufficiency and ischemia. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has long-standing swelling of both legs just above the ankles that is symmetric, with normal skin color and no ulcers or varicose veins. Cardiac, kidney and liver evaluations are normal. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Lymphedema","Heart failure edema","Lipedema","Chronic venous insufficiency"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bilateral, symmetric swelling just above the ankles in a woman, with normal cardiac, kidney and liver evaluations and no venous skin changes, is the description of lipedema in the differential of leg edema. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman eight weeks postpartum has mild calf aching and a low pretest probability of deep vein thrombosis. Her clinician orders a D-dimer. Which characteristic makes a negative result useful for excluding a clot?","choices":["High specificity","Direct visualization of clot","Measurement of vein compressibility","High sensitivity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. D-dimer is sensitive but not specific, so a negative result in a low-likelihood patient effectively rules out deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism. It cannot confirm a clot, since fibrinolysis has many causes. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man is about to undergo treatment of symptomatic varicose veins and asks about the long-term outlook. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Recurrence is rare after treatment","Only appearance improves, not symptoms","One treatment is permanently curative","New varicose veins often recur"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Varicose veins often recur after treatment, and the goal of intervention is to improve symptoms and appearance, so he should expect to follow up over the long term. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with superficial thrombophlebitis of the calf was started on compresses, elevation and an anti-inflammatory drug. At his recheck nine days later, the tender cord has lengthened and the redness has spread up the leg. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Continue the same treatment","Place an inferior vena cava filter","Reexamine again in four weeks","Repeat duplex ultrasound"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Persistence or worsening of signs and symptoms at the 7 to 10 day recheck calls for a repeat duplex ultrasound to look for extension of the clot, since the superficial clot may now need different therapy. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man receiving chemotherapy for colon cancer comes to the emergency department with three days of left calf swelling that measures 4 cm more than the right, plus tenderness along the deep veins of the calf. He has no history of clots and nothing else explains his leg. The team debates whether to order a D-dimer or go straight to imaging. Using the Wells criteria, which is the best next step?","choices":["D-dimer testing first","Contrast ascending venography","Ankle-brachial index","Venous duplex ultrasound"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Active cancer, calf swelling over 3 cm and tenderness along the deep veins total 3 points, a high likelihood. A score of 3 or more skips the D-dimer and goes directly to venous duplex ultrasound of the leg. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman seen in urgent care for mild left calf aching has a low Wells score. Her D-dimer is negative. What is the next step?","choices":["No further testing needed","Venous duplex ultrasound of the thigh","Contrast ascending venography","Repeat D-dimer in one week"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a low-likelihood patient a negative D-dimer stops the work-up, because the test is sensitive and useful for ruling out deep vein thrombosis. No ultrasound is required. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has a chronic leg ulcer whose appearance fits none of the venous, arterial or diabetic patterns. Examination and duplex ultrasound do not clarify the cause, and she has no signs of infection. Which is the best next step?","choices":["Superficial vein ablation","Catheter-based venography","Punch biopsy","Empiric oral antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When the cause of a leg ulcer is uncertain, a punch biopsy is helpful to rule out other causes such as autoimmune disease, since history and examination alone cannot exclude them. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with chronic venous insufficiency has two days of warmth, tenderness and spreading blanching redness of the lower leg, with a low-grade fever. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Oral flavonoid therapy","Oral cephalexin","Topical antibiotic ointment","Mid-potency topical corticosteroid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cellulitis complicating venous insufficiency is treated with oral antibiotics, and cephalexin or clindamycin is recommended. Topical therapy is not adequate for the infection. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has a first proximal deep vein thrombosis with no surgery, trauma, hormone use, travel, cancer or other identifiable trigger, and no inherited disorder has been found. How long should anticoagulation continue?","choices":["Three to six months","Six weeks","Indefinitely","Three months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A first venous thromboembolism with no identifiable risk factor is unprovoked, and its source, such as an undiscovered inherited clotting disorder, is unknown. Treatment therefore continues for an indefinite length of time. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has chronic leg swelling, brown discoloration from hemosiderin and a shallow ulcer above the medial ankle. Her pedal pulses are weak and her ankle-brachial index is 0.6 on that leg. Which treatment should be avoided until her arterial disease is addressed?","choices":["Skin emollients","Graduated compression","Gentle skin cleansing","Daily walking with ankle flexion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Compression is standard treatment for venous insufficiency but is contraindicated in peripheral artery disease, so the ankle-brachial index must be checked first. An index of 0.9 or less indicates disease. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man on hemodialysis has a left forearm arteriovenous fistula that was created four months ago. Last month it had a palpable thrill (a vibration felt over the access) and a soft bruit. At today's visit no thrill can be felt and no bruit is heard over the access. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat examination in 7 to 10 days","Urgent referral for access evaluation","Reassurance with routine follow-up","Warm compresses and an anti-inflammatory drug"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A healthy fistula has a diffuse thrill and a soft bruit with systolic and diastolic components. Loss of both means the access is no longer working, so it needs prompt referral to the specialist. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man with a first calf deep vein thrombosis is hemodynamically stable, has low bleeding risk and normal kidney function, and has a supportive spouse who will bring him to every follow-up visit. Which is the best setting for treatment?","choices":["Intensive care unit monitoring","Observation without treatment","Outpatient anticoagulation","Hospital admission for heparin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Outpatient therapy suits patients who are hemodynamically stable, have low bleeding risk and no renal insufficiency, and have good support with reliable follow-up. He meets every criterion. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with large varicose veins in the right leg has a painful, red, indurated cord along the medial calf that developed over two days. He is afebrile and has no swelling of the leg. Which study confirms the diagnosis and is considered the gold standard?","choices":["Venous duplex ultrasound","D-dimer assay","Contrast ascending venography","Ankle-brachial index"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Venous duplex ultrasound is the gold standard, showing a noncompressible superficial vein with clot and thickening of the vein wall. History and examination suggest the diagnosis, and the ultrasound confirms it. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with prior deep vein thrombosis has leg edema and brown pigmentation. Duplex ultrasound is performed to explain her symptoms. Which finding is expected?","choices":["Noncompressible calcified arteries","Narrowed superficial femoral artery","Dilated lymphatic channels","Incompetent venous valves with reflux"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Duplex ultrasound assesses venous patency and valve competence, and chronic venous insufficiency shows valves that fail to stop blood flowing back toward the foot, producing venous hypertension. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has a large arteriovenous fistula of the thigh that was diagnosed on physical examination. Before repair, the vascular surgeon needs the exact location and size of the connection. Which study provides this information?","choices":["Punch biopsy of the skin","Computed tomography angiography","Ankle-brachial index","Plain radiograph of the thigh"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography angiography and conventional angiography can confirm an arteriovenous fistula and give information about its location and size, helping to plan repair. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has a tender, red, firm segment along a forearm vein after a peripheral intravenous catheter was removed. Venous duplex ultrasound shows a superficial vein with a thickened wall. Which additional finding would establish thrombophlebitis rather than phlebitis alone?","choices":["Clot within the vein lumen","Thickening of the vein wall","Reflux through incompetent valves","Fluid collection around the vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Wall thickening alone is phlebitis, inflammation of the vein wall without thrombus. Thickening together with clot in the lumen is thrombophlebitis, and the distinction matters because treatment differs. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with heart failure is admitted with a tender, swollen left calf, and duplex ultrasound confirms deep vein thrombosis. Her daughter asks why heart failure raises the risk of a clot. Which Virchow triad component should the clinician name?","choices":["Elevated platelet count","Venous stasis","Hypercoagulable state","Endothelial trauma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure is listed under venous stasis, the Virchow component reflecting altered blood flow. A weak pump lets blood pool and slow in the veins of the legs, so clots form more readily. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with newly diagnosed deep vein thrombosis has chronic kidney disease with a creatinine clearance below 30 mL/min. Which approach should be avoided?","choices":["Overlapping heparin with warfarin","Beginning therapy as an outpatient","Admission for intravenous heparin","Adjusting heparin by lab results"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients with a creatinine clearance below 30 mL/min are hospitalized to start treatment, because renal insufficiency also excludes outpatient therapy. Starting at home is the approach to avoid. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with a first deep vein thrombosis is otherwise healthy and hemodynamically stable. The plan is oral anticoagulation monotherapy with a loading phase followed by maintenance, without any injection lead-in. Which drug fits this strategy?","choices":["Dabigatran","Edoxaban","Fondaparinux","Apixaban"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Apixaban, a direct factor Xa inhibitor, is given as oral monotherapy with a loading dose followed by a maintenance dose, one of the three major anticoagulation strategies. Rivaroxaban follows the same pattern. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman had femoral vessel puncture for an endovascular procedure two days ago. Examination of the groin shows a new bruit over the puncture site, and the surgeon suspects an iatrogenic arteriovenous fistula. Which study is used to confirm the diagnosis?","choices":["D-dimer assay","Ankle-brachial index","Plain radiograph of the groin","Duplex ultrasound"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Duplex ultrasound confirms the diagnosis in patients with a suspected iatrogenic fistula after groin access, and follow-up of the puncture site includes a complete lower extremity vascular examination. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman receiving chemotherapy through a central venous catheter has two days of right arm swelling and aching. The catheter injuring the vein wall is the endothelial trauma component of the Virchow triad. Which is the best diagnostic test?","choices":["Venous duplex ultrasound","Contrast ascending venography","Ankle-brachial index","D-dimer alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Venous duplex ultrasound of the involved extremity is the diagnostic test for deep vein thrombosis, including arm clots from central venous catheters. It has replaced venography, the old invasive gold standard. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has had untreated varicose veins for many years. He now has persistent lower leg swelling, brown skin discoloration and a shallow ulcer above the medial ankle. Untreated long-standing varicose veins can progress to which condition?","choices":["Lymphedema","Arteriovenous fistula","Chronic venous insufficiency","Peripheral artery disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Long-standing varicose veins can progress to chronic venous insufficiency, with edema, skin changes and venous ulceration from sustained venous hypertension. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman who is 34 weeks pregnant sits in a car for nine hours and develops left calf swelling and pain. Her pregnancy supplies a hypercoagulable state and the long ride supplies venous stasis. Which additional component would complete the Virchow triad?","choices":["Endothelial trauma","Venous stasis","Hypercoagulable state","Arterial thrombosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Virchow triad is venous stasis, hypercoagulable state and endothelial trauma. Pregnancy and the car ride supply two components, so the missing one is injury to the vein wall, as from trauma, surgery, burns or injection. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man seen in an outpatient clinic has had left calf pain and swelling for three days. A plaster cast was removed from his left leg yesterday after four weeks for a tibial fracture. Examination shows pitting edema confined to the left leg. He meets no other Wells criteria, and no alternative diagnosis is judged as likely as deep vein thrombosis. Which is the most appropriate next test?","choices":["D-dimer assay","Venous duplex ultrasound","Contrast ascending venography","Ankle-brachial index"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Recent plaster immobilization and pitting edema in the symptomatic leg give a Wells score of 2, a moderate pretest probability. Patients scoring 1 to 2 need a D-dimer, and a negative result rules out the diagnosis. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman who smokes and takes an oral contraceptive has mild calf aching. Her Wells score is low, and her D-dimer returns positive. What is the next step?","choices":["Venous duplex ultrasound","Empiric anticoagulation alone","Reassurance and discharge","Repeat D-dimer tomorrow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a low-likelihood patient a positive D-dimer cannot exclude deep vein thrombosis, so venous duplex ultrasound follows. A positive ultrasound makes the diagnosis; a negative one rules it out. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man has had symmetric pitting swelling of both ankles for three weeks. He started amlodipine for hypertension one month ago. There is no warmth, redness, tenderness or skin discoloration, and he has no history of heart, kidney or liver disease. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic venous insufficiency","Lymphedema","Drug-induced edema","Bilateral deep vein thrombosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Swelling that is often bilateral, without signs of inflammation, is a common adverse effect of some drugs, including dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as amlodipine. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with knee osteoarthritis felt a sudden sharp pain behind the right knee while climbing stairs, followed by severe calf discomfort and swelling. Venous duplex ultrasound shows no thrombus. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Superficial thrombophlebitis","Ruptured popliteal (Baker) cyst","Calf muscle tear","Post-thrombotic syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A ruptured popliteal (Baker) cyst produces severe, sudden calf discomfort that mimics deep vein thrombosis, and the ultrasound shows no clot. It is a standard item on the differential of a swollen calf. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man taking apixaban for deep vein thrombosis develops an acute intracranial hemorrhage. Which agent reverses the anticoagulant?","choices":["Andexanet alfa","Protamine sulfate","Idarucizumab","Vitamin K by mouth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Andexanet alfa reverses the direct factor Xa inhibitors apixaban and rivaroxaban in life-threatening or intracranial bleeding, and it is also used off-label for edoxaban. Hemorrhage is the most serious adverse effect of anticoagulation. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 68"}]};
